    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Owners can now be created with an optional `birthDate`, and the response includes `ageBand`. The full test suite passes: 10 tests, 0 failures, including the new acceptance test (born 1990-01-01 \u2192 `ADULT`).\n\n**How it works:**\n- **Storing the date:** `birthDate` is a new optional request field, saved in a new `birth_date` column on owners. I added the column to all four database schemas (H2, HSQLDB, MySQL, Postgres) and to the JDBC repository's queries.\n- **Working out the band:** `ageBand` isn't stored. It's calculated in `OwnerMapper.toAgeBand` each time an owner is returned. Age is counted in full years on the registration date (after it's moved off a weekend). Under 18 is `MINOR`, 18\u201364 is `ADULT`, and 65 or over is `SENIOR`.\n- **When it's missing:** owners without a birth date get no `ageBand`.\n- **Updates:** a PUT that includes `birthDate` changes it; if it's left out, the stored value is kept. This copies how `registrationDate` already behaves.\n- **API docs:** `openapi.yml` describes both fields. I typed `ageBand` as a plain string rather than an enum, so the generated code stays a simple `String`.\n\n**Choices worth checking:**\n- A birth date later than the registration date isn't rejected. It just comes out as `MINOR`. The spec didn't cover this; tell me if you'd rather return a 400.\n- Existing databases outside the in-memory test setup will need the `birth_date` column added before this code runs against them.",
